  <h2>Data Flow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>User Interaction</strong> — User interacts with a Portal or Shell (React SPA)</li>
    <li><strong>Edge Request</strong> — Request hits Cloudflare Edge, routed to appropriate Worker</li>
    <li><strong>Authentication</strong> — DID-based auth (Ed25519/ML-DSA-65) or JWT session</li>
    <li><strong>Processing</strong> — Worker processes request, may call D1/Durable Object/R2</li>
    <li><strong>Response</strong> — JSON or HTML returned, cached at edge when appropriate</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Security Model</h2>
  <p>The P31 stack implements a zero-trust security model:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Post-Quantum Cryptography</strong> — All signatures use NIST FIPS standards</li>
    <li><strong>DID-Based Identity</strong> — No central auth provider; users control their DIDs</li>
    <li><strong>Encrypted Storage</strong> — D1 and R2 data is encrypted at rest</li>
    <li><strong>TLS 1.3</strong> — All traffic encrypted with hybrid PQC TLS (when enabled at zone)</li>
    <li><strong>Secret Rotation</strong> — Automated rotation via kill-switch Worker</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Scalability</h2>
  <p>Built on Cloudflare's edge network, the P31 stack automatically scales to handle millions of requests. Durable Objects provide strong consistency where needed, while the edge cache handles high-throughput read operations.</p>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <div class="callout-title">Free Plan Limits</div>
    <p>The P31 stack operates within Cloudflare's Free Plan limits: 100k req/day per worker, 10 D1 databases, 5 cron triggers. The architecture is designed to work within these constraints while maintaining functionality.</p>
  </div>

